The de Broglie wavelength is given by the formula: $\lambda = \frac{h}{p} = \frac{h}{mv}$, where $h$ is Planck's constant, $m$ is the mass, and $v$ is the velocity.

(a) Truck:
$m = 10000 \text{ kg}$, $v = 27.8 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{(10000 \text{ kg})(27.8 \text{ m/s})} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{2.78 \times 10^6} \text{ m} = 2.30 \times 10^{-40} \text{ m} = 2.30 \times 10^{-38} \text{ cm}$

(b) Flea:
$m = 50 \text{ mg} = 50 \times 10^{-3} \text{ g} = 50 \times 10^{-6} \text{ kg} = 5.0 \times 10^{-5} \text{ kg}$, $v = 1 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{(5.0 \times 10^{-5} \text{ kg})(1 \text{ m/s})} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{5.0 \times 10^{-5}} \text{ m} = 1.325 \times 10^{-29} \text{ m} = 1.325 \times 10^{-27} \text{ cm}$

(c) Water molecule:
$m = 18 \text{ amu} = 18 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} = 2.988 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg}$, $v = 500 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{(2.988 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg})(500 \text{ m/s})} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{1.494 \times 10^{-23}} \text{ m} = 4.43 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m} = 4.43 \times 10^{-9} \text{ cm}$

The wavelengths are approximately 2.30 x 10^-38 cm, 1.325 x 10^-27 cm, and 4.43 x 10^-9 cm. Looking at the options, the closest is option E: 2.4 × 10^-37 cm, 1.3 × 10^-27 cm, 4.43 × 10^-9 cm.

